://arxiv.org/abs/2510.11696)\nciteturn3academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Beyond efficiency, our findings show that quantization noise increases policy entropy, enhancing exploration, and enabling the discovery of better strategies during RL. ... Experiments demonstrate that QeRL delivers over 1.5 times speedup in the rollout phase. ... It also achieves faster reward growth and higher final accuracy than 16-bit LoRA and QLoRA, while matching the performance of full-parameter fine-tuning on mathematical benchmarks such as GSM8K (90.8%) and MATH 500 (77.4%) in the 7B model.\nTitle: QeRL: Beyond Efficiency -- Quantization-enhanced Reinforcement Learning for LLMs\nAuthors: Wei Huang, Yi Ge, Shuai Yang, Yicheng Xiao, Huizi Mao, Yujun Lin, Hanrong Ye, Sifei Liu, Ka Chun Cheung, Hongxu Yin, Yao Lu, Xiaojuan Qi, Song Han, Yukang Chen\nDate: Mon Oct 13 17:55:09 2025\n\nWe propose QeRL, a Quantization-enhanced Reinforcement Learning framework for large language models (LLMs). While RL is essential for LLMs' reasoning capabilities, it is resource-intensive, requiring substantial GPU memory and long rollout durations. QeRL addresses these issues by combining NVFP4 quantization with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), accelerating rollout phase of RL while reducing memory overhead. Beyond efficiency, our findings show that quantization noise increases policy entropy, enhancing exploration, and enabling the discovery of better strategies during RL. To further optimize exploration, QeRL introduces an Adaptive Quantization Noise (AQN) mechanism, which dynamically adjusts noise during training. Experiments demonstrate that QeRL delivers over 1.5 times speedup in the rollout phase. Moreover, this is the first framework to enable RL training of a 32B LLM on a single H100 80GB GPU, while delivering overall speedups for RL training. It also achieves faster reward growth and higher final accuracy than 16-bit LoRA and QLoRA, while matching the performance of full-parameter fine-tuning on mathematical benchmarks such as GSM8K (90.8%) and MATH 500 (77.4%) in the 7B model. These results establish QeRL as an efficient and effective framework for RL training in LLMs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n18  Matching-Adjusted Indirect Comparison – The Mathematics of Indirect Treatment Comparisons (https://choxos.github.io/Mathematics-of-ITC/chapters/part4/18-maic.html)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; In the running Example 18.1, of all the weightings that pull the mean severity up to \\(0.5\\), entropy balancing chose the mildest one, \\((1.5,1.5,0.5,0.5)\\).\nThe objective line (\\(\\min\\sum_i w_i\\log w_i\\)) is the tie-breaker among the infinitely many ways to meet that requirement. Its plain meaning is “distort the original trial as little as possible.” We want to reweight just enough to fix the covariate mismatch and no more, because every extra distortion piles emphasis onto a few patients, overfits to their idiosyncrasies, and inflates the standard error. Entropy is the mathematical name for “how far the weights stray from equal”; minimizing it keeps the pseudo-population as close as it can be to the trial we actually ran while still hitting the target. In the running Example 18.1, of all the weightings that pull the mean severity up to \\(0.5\\), entropy balancing chose the mildest one, \\((1.5,1.5,0.5,0.5)\\).\n\nRemark. Why this objective is the KL divergence from uniform. Write the normalized weights \\(p_i=w_i/\\sum_j w_j\\), so \\(\\sum_i p_i=1\\). The KL divergence of \\(\\mathbf{p}\\) from the uniform distribution \\(\\mathbf{u}=(1/n,\\dots,1/n)\\) is \\[ \\mathrm{KL}(\\mathbf{p}\\,\\|\\,\\mathbf{u})=\\sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i\\log\\frac{p_i}{1/n} =\\sum_{i=1}^{n} p_i\\log p_i+\\log n. \\tag{18.4}\\] Under the normalization \\(\\sum_i w_i=n\\) we have \\(p_i=w_i/n\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNearly Complete Graph